<header>
  <h1>Middle Ear Ventilation</h1>
  <p>Theoretical Models and Research</p>
  <p>Dr. Mihaly Fucsek</p>
</header>

<main>
  <h2>Research Overview</h2>
  <p>
    This website presents theoretical research on
    the physiological mechanisms of middle ear
    ventilation and gas exchange.
  </p>

  <p>
    The proposed model examines the dynamic
    interaction between continuous transmucosal
    gas diffusion and intermittent Eustachian
    tube ventilation.
  </p>

  <h2>Physiological Principles</h2>
  <p>
    Middle ear ventilation has two important
    functions: maintaining pressure relative
    to the surrounding atmosphere and regulating
    the gas composition of the middle ear cavity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Oxygen and nitrogen exchange with surrounding
    tissues through diffusion driven by their
    partial-pressure gradients.
    Intermittent opening of the Eustachian tube
    allows pressure-driven gas exchange with
    the nasopharynx.
  </p>

  <h2>Dynamic Equilibrium</h2>
  <p>
    The research proposes that the interaction
    of these processes can establish a dynamic
    equilibrium of middle ear gases.
    Mathematical modelling is used to explore
    this hypothesis.
  </p>

  <h2>Published Research</h2>
  <p>
    Fucsek M, Molnarka GY (2022).
    <em>A Mathematical Model of Middle Ear
    Ventilation. Controlled Valvular
    Tympanostomy Tube.</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    Journal of Biomedical Research and
    Clinical Reviews, 7(1).
  </p>
  <p>
    DOI:
    <a href="https://doi.org/10.31579/2690-4861/122">
      10.31579/2690-4861/122
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Proposed Valvular Tympanostomy Tube</h2>
  <p>
    A theoretical passive, bidirectional,
    pressure-controlled tympanostomy tube
    is proposed to provide intermittent
    pressure equalization rather than
    permanent open ventilation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The concept remains theoretical and
    requires experimental validation.
  </p>

